#import "../../Zusatz/acronyms.typ": acronyms

#acrfpl("CT") provide the current signals that power-system protection depends on. The software used to dimension them must therefore produce results that are correct, traceable and suitable for delivery to the customer. This project evaluates the #acrs("CT") adequacy calculations of #acrs("SECP") Global, an internal substation-engineering tool, for use in the planning phase. The work is set up as an evaluation study within design-science research and organized as a single-case study. The central release question is broken down into four research questions covering numerical correctness, report traceability, coverage of the required devices and report fitness for delivery. Each question is answered with a defined metric and the overall verdict follows a non-compensatory decision rule. Every finding is recorded with a three-level defect classification that links the software cause, the numerical effect and the #acrs("IEC") 61869 adequacy benchmark. #acrs("SECP") is compared case by case against an independent reference model derived from #acrs("IEC") 61869-2, #acrs("IEC") TR 61869-100 and the applicable device manuals.

Three application–device cases are examined: Transformer differential protection with the RET670, line differential protection with the RED670 and overcurrent protection with a REF650 requirement. The line differential results agree closely with the reference and can be released once a small conservative deviation is disclosed. For transformer differential protection the suitability decisions match the reference, but the required value itself is numerically incorrect, because the tool counts the secondary lead resistance twice. This defect is conservative in direction, yet the printed value departs from the device requirement the report cites. The overcurrent case cannot be computed with the required device at all: The REF650 is not selectable and the offered REF630 substitute uses a different dimensioning basis, which leaves a coverage gap. In addition, every generated report cites a superseded edition of #acrs("IEC") 61869-1. Under the non-compensatory decision rule, #acrs("SECP") Global is therefore not releasable without qualification for the planning-phase use case in its present state. For line differential protection, however, it can already be used once the identified improvements are addressed.
